    "innovation": "This paper quantifies the maximum sustainable technical potential of biochar for climate change mitigation, estimating it could reduce annual net emissions by up to 1.8 Pg CO2-C equivalent (12% of current anthropogenic emissions) without endangering food security, habitat, or soil conservation.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "BGRAM version 1.1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "net avoided GHG emissions",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "A model (BGRAM version 1.1) to calculate the net avoided GHG emissions attributable to sustainable biochar production as a function of time was developed and applied to the three scenarios.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "BGRAM version 1.1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "carbon-payback time",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "In addition to its negative effects on ecosystem conservation, land clearance to provide feedstock may also release carbon stored in soils and biomass, leading to unacceptably high carbon-payback times before any net reduction in atmospheric CO2 is achieved (ref. 25, Supplementary Methods and Supplementary Fig. S2). For example, we find that a land-use change carbon debt greater than 22 MgCha\u2212\u00b9 (an amount that would be exceeded by conversion of temperate grassland to annual crops25) will result in a carbon-payback time that is greater than 10 years. Clearance of rainforests to provide land for biomass-crop production leads to carbon payback times in excess of 50 years. Where rainforest on peatland is converted to biomass-crop production, carbon-payback times may be in the order of 325 years.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "BGRAM version 1.1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "cumulative avoided emissions",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The model predicts that maximum avoided emissions of 1.0\u20131.8 Pg CO2-C, per year are approached by mid-century and that, after a century, the cumulative avoided emissions are 66\u2013130 Pg CO\u2082-C (Fig. 2).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "BGRAM version 1.1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USED_IN_EXPERIMENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "MSTP scenario",
            "target_entity_type": "Experiment",
            "paragraph": "Within these constraints, we derived a biomass-availability scenario for our estimate of MSTP, as well as two additional scenarios, Alpha and Beta, which represent lower demands on global biomass resources (Table 1). ... A model (BGRAM version 1.1) to calculate the net avoided GHG emissions attributable to sustainable biochar production as a function of time was developed and applied to the three scenarios.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
